                new(""a1-v2"", ""assistant"", ""Think of JavaScript as a restaurant with one chef (single thread).\n\nThe kitchen analogy:\n- The call stack is the chef's current task \u2014 chop onions, boil water, plate food\n- Web APIs are the ovens and timers \u2014 they run on their own\n- The callback queue is the order rail \u2014 finished items waiting to be plated\n- The event loop is the chef glancing at the rail: \""Am I free? Let me grab the next thing.\""\n\nThe chef can only do one thing at a time, but the ovens keep cooking in the background. That's why fetch() doesn't freeze your UI.\n\nMicrotasks vs Macrotasks: Promises (microtasks) cut the line. They're processed before the next setTimeout/setInterval (macrotasks).""),
