    private static readonly ChatMessage[] _messages =
    {
        new(""1"", ""user"", ""What is the difference between composition and inheritance in software design?""),
        new(""2"", ""assistant"", ""Inheritance creates an \""is-a\"" relationship. A Dog is an Animal. You get the parent's behavior automatically, but you're locked into that hierarchy. Change the parent, everything downstream shifts.\n\nComposition creates a \""has-a\"" relationship. A Dog has a BarkBehavior, has a WalkBehavior. You assemble objects from small, focused pieces. Swap one piece without touching the rest.\n\nThe practical difference: inheritance is rigid, composition is flexible. With inheritance you define what something is. With composition you define what something does.\n\nMost modern codebases favor composition. React hooks are composition. Go interfaces are composition. The Gang of Four said it in 1994: favor composition over inheritance.\n\nUse inheritance when you have a genuine taxonomic hierarchy that won't change. Use composition for everything else.""),
        new(""3"", ""user"", ""Can you show this with a concrete example?""),
        new(""4"", ""assistant"", ""Say you're building a notification system.\n\nInheritance approach:\n\n  EmailNotification extends Notification\n  SMSNotification extends Notification\n  SlackNotification extends Notification\n\nNow you need a notification that sends email AND Slack. You can't extend both. You're stuck.\n\nComposition approach:\n\n  Notification has channels: Channel[]\n  EmailChannel implements Channel\n  SMSChannel implements Channel\n  SlackChannel implements Channel\n\nNeed email + Slack? Create a Notification with both channels. Need to add a webhook channel? Write one more class, plug it in. Nothing else changes.\n\nThe composition version handles every combination without modifying existing code. That's the open-closed principle in action.""),
        new(""5"", ""user"", ""When would you actually choose inheritance then?""),
        new(""6"", ""assistant"", ""Three situations where inheritance still makes sense:\n\nFirst, framework contracts. React class components extend Component. Django views extend View. The framework defines the shape, you fill in the specifics. This rarely changes.\n\nSecond, genuine type hierarchies that are stable. AST nodes in a parser: Expression, Statement, Declaration. These map directly to a language spec that doesn't move.\n\nThird, when you need polymorphism with shared state. A base class with constructor logic, shared fields, and a few overridable methods. Composition can do this too, but sometimes a simple two-level hierarchy is clearer than wiring together three interfaces.\n\nThe test is simple: will this hierarchy ever need multiple inheritance? Will the base class change often? If either answer is yes, use composition."")
    };

    private string _input = """";
}";

