        [""blog""] = new(""Why TypeScript Is Winning the Enterprise"",
            ""The enterprise adoption of TypeScript has accelerated dramatically over the past three years. What started as a Microsoft side project has become the default choice for large-scale JavaScript applications.\n\nTeams at companies like Airbnb, Slack, and Bloomberg have reported significant improvements after migrating to TypeScript. Airbnb documented a 38% reduction in production bugs after their migration, while Slack's team noted that onboarding new developers became measurably faster.\n\nThe key advantages driving adoption include:\n\nType Safety at Scale\n\nAs codebases grow beyond 100,000 lines, the absence of types becomes a liability. TypeScript catches entire categories of bugs at compile time that would otherwise surface in production.\n\nBetter Developer Experience\n\nModern editors like VS Code provide intelligent autocompletion, inline documentation, and refactoring tools that rely on type information. This alone saves hours of developer time per week.\n\nIncremental Adoption\n\nUnlike previous attempts at typed JavaScript, TypeScript can be adopted file-by-file. Teams don't need to rewrite their entire codebase to start seeing benefits.\n\nThe data speaks clearly: according to the 2025 State of JS survey, 89% of developers who use TypeScript would use it again, making it the most-loved language in the JavaScript ecosystem.\n\nFor teams still on the fence, the question is no longer \""Should we adopt TypeScript?\"" but rather \""How quickly can we migrate?\"""",
