            ""Evaluating optimization strategies by impact and effort:\n\n1. React.memo + useMemo + useCallback \u2014 High impact if they have prop drilling or expensive computations. But over-memoizing is a common pitfall that actually hurts performance.\n\n2. Code splitting with dynamic imports \u2014 Medium-high impact. Reduces initial bundle size. Worth it for route-level splitting at minimum.\n\n3. Virtualization for long lists \u2014 Critical if they're rendering 100+ items. React-window or TanStack Virtual are the go-to solutions.\n\n4. Moving state closer to where it's used \u2014 Often the simplest fix. If a parent re-renders, all children re-render. Colocating state prevents cascading re-renders.\n\n5. Server Components (if Next.js) \u2014 Zero JS shipped for non-interactive UI. This is the biggest win for Next.js apps.\n\nI should prioritize by what typically gives the biggest wins with the least effort...""),
        new(""step-3"", ""Synthesizing the answer"", 4,
            ""Structuring the response around practical, high-impact recommendations:\n\n1. Start with the React DevTools Profiler \u2014 they need to measure before optimizing\n2. Server Components first (if applicable) \u2014 biggest single win\n3. State colocation \u2014 simple, often overlooked\n4. Memoization with caveats \u2014 useful but don't overdo it\n5. Virtualization for lists \u2014 specific but very impactful\n6. Code splitting \u2014 easy wins with dynamic imports\n\nI'll include code examples for each recommendation and mention the common mistake of over-optimizing. The \""measure first\"" principle is important to emphasize."")
