    private const string FinalAnswer = ""Here are the 6 most impactful React performance optimizations, ordered by typical ROI:\n\n1. Measure first with React DevTools Profiler\nDon't guess \u2014 profile your app to find the actual bottlenecks. The Profiler shows exactly which components re-render and how long each render takes.\n\n2. Use Server Components (Next.js)\nIf you're on Next.js, this is your biggest win. Server Components send zero JavaScript to the client. Default to server components and only add \""use client\"" when you need interactivity.\n\n3. Colocate state\nMove state as close to where it's used as possible. If only a search bar needs query state, don't put it in a top-level context that re-renders the entire page.\n\n4. Memoize strategically\nUse React.memo, useMemo, and useCallback for expensive computations and components that receive complex props. But don't wrap everything \u2014 memoization has its own cost.\n\n5. Virtualize long lists\nIf you're rendering 100+ items, use react-window or @@tanstack/react-virtual. Only the visible items get rendered, reducing DOM nodes dramatically.\n\n6. Code-split with dynamic imports\nSplit heavy components and routes with next/dynamic or React.lazy. Users only download the JavaScript they actually need.\n\nStart with steps 1-3 \u2014 they're the easiest wins and usually solve 80% of performance issues."";

